  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 AM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nPattern / Forecast Synopsis: \n\nStout surface high right over our heads at the time of this \ndiscussion's writing, with ample subsidence leading to minimal low \nlevel moisture. Return flow aloft commencing, which has drawn some \nhigh cloud into the region. Meanwhile, a double barrel wave setup \nbetween a northern stream wave over the northern Plains / Canadian \nPrairies and a southern stream wave over Texas will both move toward \nthe lower Ohio Valley, with phasing commencing across the Mid \nMississippi and lower Ohio valleys into Thanksgiving Day. Result will \nbe a cold-core closed low that will drift north and east into the \nlower Great Lakes, with surface cyclogenesis generating an other \ncoastal low off the Carolinas. This complex system will quickly lift \nout of the region despite its pseudo-cutoff nature. By the weekend, \nthe northern and southern streams will go back to being split, with \na series of several moisture starved northern stream waves \ntraversing the Great Lakes through the conclusion of the forecast \nperiod next week.\n\nDetails: \n\nLooking dry both today and much of Thursday with some high clouds \nintruding ahead of the next system. Near to just above normal \ntemperatures to prevail both days. Highs in the 30s today with highs \nin the 40s for Thanksgiving Day. Ample low level dry air will make \nprecipitation development difficult to manifest as moisture \nadvection. Majority of the deep moisture concentration with this \nsystem will also pass to our south and east, with northern Michigan \nlocated on the very northwester periphery of the system. As such, \nonce the better lift and highest moisture concentration associated \nwith the system moves overhead, anticipating the most frequent \nprecipitation to favor areas east of I-75 / US 127 in northern lower \n(especailly the closer one goes toward Lake Huron / Saginaw Bay), \nwith lesser precipitation anticipated across NW lower and eastern \nupper. \n\nSuppose there is potential for some snowflakes to mix in across the \ninterior terrain Thursday night into Friday as precipitation moves \nin, but with surface temps largely in the mid 30s, don't really see \nan overwhelming impact other than snowflakes being visible to the \nhuman eye. Highest QPF across the region will certainly be along the \nUS 23 corridor from roughly Rogers City through Arenac County. \nSuppose some places see 0.25\"+ of rain, but overall, probably \nsomewhere between 0.10-0.25\". Up to 0.10\" seems reasonable between I-\n75 and M-33, and west of I-75 might even be lucky to pick up \n0.05\"... and even precip in general the closer one gets to Lake \nMichigan in NW lower and Whitefish Point in eastern upper.  \n\nRest of the forecast period looks quite mundane as well. With an \noverall lack of moisture and deeper arctic cold (highs in the 40s, \nlows in the 20s and 30s) and a series of quick-hitting northern \nstream waves, light rain / snow mix seems reasonable at this point, \nbut again, not anticipating anything earth-shattering. Timing of \nthese waves: Saturday night into Sunday, and again closer to midweek \nnext week.",
